<p>Sudan’s military said it downed a drone launched by the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces targeting Khartoum’s main airport, as drone attacks hit the capital after months of relative calm. Sudan Tribune reported RSF drone attacks on several Khartoum-area targets beyond the civilian airport. The attack prompted a diplomatic dispute involving Ethiopia and the United Arab Emirates, according to Reuters and Sudanese state media reports carried by SCMP, The Straits Times and Eyewitness News.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/l1zu3T5M82fp5xqX_usAA8PkIRUIcmic5tG2K9oaW_kdt3wIrAwPryL7c7-Es8jB7MLpBwte20T6_os4o5MPE3IeitgctZRCNj0-IxA_t4Ag3SsnudTDIEPkL1zaPx8kf-bd82mH8waqfKMwo1Dfxo-z8uRNNdKKpyxWFhjvvw' alt='Image distributed with ABC News report on Sudan’s military saying it downed a drone targeting Khartoum airport.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Witness accounts: Witnesses in the Amarat and Burri neighborhoods near the airport reported loud explosions and smoke rising from inside the airport.</li><li>Attack timing: Sky News Arabia reported that the airport strike occurred around midday Sunday, alongside attacks near the army’s military area in Omdurman, northwest of the capital.</li><li>Airport location: Sky News Arabia and Al Maghrib Today described Khartoum airport as east of the capital.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sudanese armed forces: The army blamed the airport drone attack on the United Arab Emirates and Ethiopia. Reuters said it could not independently verify the allegation. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3352452/drones-shatter-months-relative-calm-sudans-capital-international-airport-targeted'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Airport operations official: An official with a company operating at the airport said staff were surprised by the attack and evacuated work crews. (<a href='https://www.almaghribtoday.net/36/144133-%D9%87%D8%AC%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A8%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A6%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%85%D8%B3%D9%8A%D8%B1%D8%A9-%